# Milō backend — the privileged commands the service (running as `milo`) may run
# without a password. This file is the single authority: the pi-gen stage copies
# it verbatim rather than restating it. It used to be an inline heredoc, which is
# how a policy and its copy come to disagree silently.
#
# Every command here has a caller in backend/, and every `sudo` the backend
# issues is listed here — both directions are enforced by
# backend/tests/contracts/test_privileged_exec_contract.py.

# Suppress sudo + PAM session logs (noisy in journalctl)
Defaults:milo !syslog, !pam_session

# What the grants below amount to: the backend is root by construction. A bare
# `systemctl` takes any argv (`link`, `edit --full`), milo-deploy-update installs
# as root a checkout `milo` can write, and milo-set-password asks for no old
# password. Scoping one of them closes nothing while the other two stand, so they
# stay unscoped on purpose. The boundary is elsewhere: the LAN, since nothing on
# the API authenticates (api/system.py::set_ssh_state), and NoNewPrivileges=true
# on every other unit running as `milo`, which keeps a compromised daemon out of
# this file (backend/tests/architecture/test_unit_hardening.py). Its limits: a
# process of the same uid can still ptrace the backend, and the units without
# ProtectHome (kiosk, snapserver, snapclient, camilladsp, navidrome) can still
# write the checkout the backend and milo-navidrome-config (root) run.

# Take back the image's blanket grant, where the image has one. A Raspberry Pi
# OS base may ship /etc/sudoers.d/010_pi-nopasswd (a conffile of
# raspberrypi-sys-mods) whose username userconf-pi rewrites on first boot; on a
# unit flashed from such a base — pi-gen/config sets FIRST_USER_NAME="milo" — it
# reads `milo ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL` and the grants below are decorative. The
# current base ships none: re-read 2026-11-07, this server (raspberrypi-sys-mods
# 1:20260612) has no such file, and a satellite reflashed the day before asks
# `milo` for a password. The rule stays, since nothing here pins the base and a
# conffile can come back. sudoers applies the *last* matching rule and reads
# /etc/sudoers.d in lexical order, so this file (m…) is read after that one
# (0…). Same rule, same reasoning, in milo-client/rootfs/etc/sudoers.d/milo-client,
# where it was measured, and in milo-account, which carries it for `milo` on a
# satellite once the conversion has removed this file.
#
# Order is load-bearing: it must stay above the grants, or it becomes the last
# match for those too and the backend loses every privileged command it has.
milo ALL=(ALL:ALL) PASSWD: ALL

# System control (SystemdServiceManager: start/stop/restart a unit, and
# `systemctl reboot|poweroff` for the power path — there is no separate
# /usr/sbin/reboot grant because nothing shells those binaries)
milo ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/systemctl
# Hardware configuration
milo ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/milo-apply-hardware
# Update deployment (file ops, packages, udev — all via secure wrapper)
milo ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/milo-deploy-update
# WiFi regulatory domain
milo ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/milo-set-wifi-country
# Account password for SSH + sudo (reads the password on stdin, never argv)
milo ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/milo-set-password
# System timezone
milo ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/milo-set-timezone
# Music Library USB storage (read-only mount / unmount under /media/milo)
milo ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/milo-mount
milo ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/bin/milo-umount
